      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "The relationship between metabolic syndrome (MetS), and the development of post-thrombotic syndrome (PTS) is currently unknown."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "We enrolled 120 patients with a previous episode of deep venous thrombosis (DVT) diagnosed more than 2years apart from the enrollment. Presence of MetS was identified according to NCEP ATP III criteria and Villalta Score (VS) was used to establish the presence of PTS (VS≥5)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "We identified 49 (40.8%) subjects with clinical diagnosed of PTS. Patients with or without PTS showed comparable age and temporal distance from DVT event. We observed higher BMI (p=0.005) and waist circumference (p=0.006) among subjects with VS≥5 as compared to patients without PTS. No differences between the two groups were found in terms of lipid profile, blood pressure, diabetes, hs-CRP level and ongoing medications. The prevalence of MetS was equally distributed among patients with or without PTS (20% vs 26% respectively, p=0.64). Among the individual components of MetS only the prevalence of visceral adiposity was significantly increased in subjects affected by PTS (OR 2.81, p=0.008). Moreover, a significant linear correlation was found between VS and waist circumference in the entire cohort (r=0,354, p<0.0001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "There is no evidence of association between MetS and PTS. However, the degree of visceral adiposity is strongly correlated with the presence and severity of post-thrombotic disease."
          }
        ]
      },
